A||||15||batboy||Lower, firmer, tougher, meaner||||6
Z||000000||batboy||10-24-1999||02:51 PM||||It's been a long drawn out project, but the Batmobile front end is done... well... sort of... I have to readjust the tie rods, it's terribly out of alignment. The front wheels both point out slightly. I didn't realize the replacement inner rods were a tad longer. I'm going to adjust the tie rods this afternoon between football games.<P>However, I'm very pleased with the ST sport lowering springs. I put slightly smaller tires on too, 215-60-14 (235-60-14 on the back), so I have a total drop of the Fiero's nose of just over 2 inches. It might settle a tad more after driving it more. I did drive it about a mile down a bumpy gravel road. Handled very tight, no hint of coming close to bottoming out, firm suspension, but not too stiff. I like it so far.<P>Of course I have to get a four wheel alignment, before I can do real test driving. But, before I can do that, I have to replace the rear control arm bushings with my new prothane poly bushings and install new rear ball joints. That'll be another weekend project in itself for me.<P>The Batmobile front end project: a pair of sport lowering front springs (Suspension Techniques - $70), poly control arm bushings (Prothane - $47), poly sway bar bushings (Energy Suspensions - $14), poly sway bar end-link kit (Energy Suspensions - $15), 2 upper and 2 lower ball joints (Moog - $100), 2 inner and 2 outer tie rod ends (Moog - $170), steering stabilizer shock (Moog - $32), wheel bearing were already done during the brake rebuild (4 Federal-Mogul bearings and 2 inner seals - $40). By the time I figure in a couple of new bolts and grease for the ball joints and tie rods... It turns out to be about a $500 total front end rebuild. I still need an alignment too.||||reg||6
Z||000001||lowCG||10-24-1999||06:45 PM||||Glad to hear it's coming together to a point where you can see/feel some serious improvement.Did you notice a big difference,and if so,how with the steering stabilizer shock?<BR>Never had a functioning one in any of my cars(that had them),and there was always something else to replace...||||reg||1
Z||000002||DXR_DAD||10-24-1999||08:44 PM||||Batboy I've heard that the FS sport springs are just cut stock fiero springs (?) what is the spring rate of (ST) yours if you know. Is there any differance in the two?||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||10-24-1999||10:05 PM||||LowCG, the old stabilizer was completely worn out. The new Moog replacement stabilizer is twice the diameter of the original one, looks massive. I figured since I was rebuilding everything, I'd go ahead and replace it too.<P>Dad, I wish I knew the spring rate of these ST springs. I can't find it listed anywhere on the box. I didn't see it listed on their website either. But, they're definitely a stiffer spring rate, no doubt about it. About 3 inches shorter than stock when they're off the car. The springs looks like they're vinyl coated. They were a perfect fit and lowered the front the advertised 1-1/2 inches. So far, so good. I'll get a picture of the front end of the Batmobile posted Monday night (maybe).<P>Well, I adjusted the tie rods some more. It's better than it was before, but still sadly out of alignment. If I'm careful, it should at least drive to the shop to get the alignment done, once I get the rear polyurethane bushings and rear ball joints done.<P>I'm now considering lowering the rear a little bit too. I'm seriously considering coil overs in the rear. Ryane Motor Sports has their coil over kits for $264. Is there a better bargain or another option I'm missing? Seems like those coil overs should be lots cheaper. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||10-24-1999||10:40 PM||||I found another site that sells rear coil overs for Fieros. At $214, they're a little cheaper than Ryane's.<P> <A HREF="http://www.shelbycustom.com/r-coil/r-coil.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.shelbycustom.com/r-coil/r-coil.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||lowCG||10-24-1999||11:57 PM||||I believe the Citation strut housings have the spring seat at a lower point,maybe an inch or so,have threaded collars for cartridge replacement also.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Phil||10-25-1999||05:09 AM||||If you are handy with a cut off wheel and a welder you can take the cartriges out of your  stock struts cut off the spring perches and reweld them an inch or so lower on your struts and reassemble. The big advantage of a  system like Ryane's is your choice of spring rates and they are adjustable for height. I have Rayne on my KitCar and they are a quality item but pricey.||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||10-25-1999||08:04 AM||||Thanks LowCG and Phil. Now that's what I was hoping to hear. Some other options that I hadn't thought about. I like the Citation strut idea, which means less modification. I'm assuming you need to get a pair of front Citation struts housings. What about the hats? You still use the Fiero springs right? Would it just be better to grab the entire assembl?||||reg||1
Z||000008||DJRice||10-25-1999||09:14 AM||||Batboy, When do we get the picks of the new and improved batmobile?||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||10-25-1999||10:17 AM||||I had to buy new batteries for my digital camera or else the pics would of already been posted. Look for a pic tonight after I get home.||||reg||1
Z||000010||lowCG||10-25-1999||01:21 PM||||Yeah,you would need to make sure and get a strut cartridge that has the same configuration on top,so you can use your mounts.If I had a Gabriel book in front of me,I'd be able and find one,the picture guide/buyers guide in the back is great for finding things like that.That's how I found my front Bilstien application.<BR>As far as junkyard parts are concerned,the housings are all you'd need,and they use a pretty common cartridge length.||||reg||1
Z||000011||batboy||10-25-1999||08:56 PM||||The Batmobile is the silver 85 Fiero and for comparison, I stuck my black 85 Fiero GT with a factory suspension in the pic too. Look at the difference in the fender wheel well. The different shaped noses are kind of deceiving. Tires are exactly the same size on the front of both cars (215-60-14). I think that the heavy V8 in the rear also made the front end seem too high before this 2 inch drop.<P> <IMG SRC="http://sites.netscape.net/batboybain/batnosex.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||batboy||10-25-1999||09:12 PM||||Here is a "before" shot of the Batmobile. Look at those front fender wheel wells. I could of put 4X4 tires on the front of the car back then. The light blue truck behind the Fiero is a 36 Ford streetrod.<P> <IMG SRC="http://sites.netscape.net/batboybain/batn36f.jpg"> <p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 10-25-1999).]||||reg||6
Z||000013||theogre||10-26-1999||02:05 PM||||don't forget to reaim your headlights. they could be quite a bit shorter pattern with the 2" front drop. Might only be a degree or 2 off at the light, but that can be several feet at 20 yards.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||batboy||10-26-1999||08:12 PM||||Ogre, you're right, thanks. I hadn't thought about the headlights yet. Sure would of noticed it the first time I used my lights though. I can adjust them somewhat out in the driveway.||||reg||1
Z||000015||batboy||10-27-1999||09:11 PM||||Everytime I look at the Batmobile now, I like the way the front sits better and better. Gotta start thinking about adding a front air dam and a rear sway bar now.||||reg||1
